    id: "6e372ec638faa4d199d0",
    cat: "conflitti",
    title: "Qatar, France call for opening of Hormuz and Gaza ceasefire implementation",
    summary: "Qatari and French leaders stress need to restore freedom of navigation in Strait of Hormuz.",
    preview: "Qatar and France have called for freedom of navigation in the Strait of Hormuz and the implementation of the so-called ceasefire in Gaza, after the Emir of Qatar, Sheikh Tamim bin Hamad Al Thani, met French President Emmanuel Macron in Doha. A joint statement on Wednesday said the two leaders stressed the importance of restoring freedom of navigation in the Strait of Hormuz “without constraints or fees”.",
    body: "Qatar and France have called for freedom of navigation in the Strait of Hormuz and the implementation of the so-called ceasefire in Gaza, after the Emir of Qatar, Sheikh Tamim bin Hamad Al Thani, met French President Emmanuel Macron in Doha.\n\nA joint statement on Wednesday said the two leaders stressed the importance of restoring freedom of navigation in the Strait of Hormuz “without constraints or fees”.\n\nRecommended Stories\n\nlist of 3 items\n\n- list 1 of 3Macron lines up more Gulf diesel as France battles soaring fuel prices\n\n- list 2 of 3As Macron visits Gulf, can France forge its own energy-for-security future?\n\n- list 3 of 3Iran’s enriched uranium re-emerges as point of contention in US talks\n\nQatari mediator Ibrahim bin Sultan Al Hashmi had said on Tuesday that diplomatic efforts are ongoing to de-escalate regional tensions and reopen the Strait of Hormuz, while Iran’s Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi said on Wednesday that “Iran is prepared to give diplomacy another chance.”\n\n“The two leaders condemned Iranian military attacks against countries in the region which constitute a serious breach of international law,” according to the Qatari-French statement.\n\nHeeding ‘agreed arrangements’\n\nThe two leaders also called for the withdrawal of Israeli forces from Gaza “in accordance with the agreed arrangements”, as well as the urgent, safe and unhindered delivery of humanitarian aid “without conditions or restrictions”.\n\nThe United Nations World Food Programme (WFP) said on Monday that about 1.4 million people in Gaza, roughly two-thirds of the population, face acute food insecurity at crisis levels or worse.\n\n“France and Qatar reiterated their opposition to any annexation of the West Bank and called for an end to Israeli settlement policies and settler violence,” the statement added.\n\nOn Lebanon, the two countries backed full implementation of the Trilateral Framework Agreement to restore Lebanese sovereignty, disarm Hezbollah and secure a complete Israeli withdrawal.\n\nThey also warned against a security vacuum following the end of the UN peacekeeping mission’s mandate, calling for continued international support for Lebanese authorities and armed forces.\n\nOn Syria, both countries said they were ready to jointly support stabilisation and economic recovery projects in coordination with Syrian authorities.\n\nBeyond regional issues, Qatar and France agreed to expand defence, security and economic cooperation.\n\nMacron’s regional tour, which also included Kuwait, the United Arab Emirates, Bahrain and Saudi Arabia, aimed to strengthen strategic and economic partnerships and support efforts to restore regional stability and freedom of navigation through the Strait of Hormuz.",
    content_status: "full",
    source: "Al Jazeera",
    url: "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/22/qatar-france-call-for-opening-of-hormuz-and-gaza-ceasefire-implementation?traffic_source=rss",
